Changelog: 13-10-2026 11:02:38

Controller test and the account ID from the clipboard (owner, 13-10-2026). Controller test…, next to Steam's PS button setting: every input of the controllers on the board live (the 16 buttons, each ticked once pressed, with a count; both sticks drawn with a 10 % circle that a stick at rest should come back into; L2 and R2; the touchpad's fingers; gyro and accelerometer), each controller's details from SDL (USB ID, firmware where SDL knows it, cable or wireless, battery, sensors, touchpad, rumble, light bar), Rumble left, Rumble right and Light bar for the selected controller, so a broken controller shows before the app is suspected. It reads the controllers as the stream does (gamepad.Reader, which gets a watch callback, the controllers' details and battery, and outputs for one controller), and is not opened during a stream. genuine.py: whether a Sony DualShock 4 or DualSense answers like a genuine one, by reading (only) its feature reports through hidraw in the kernel driver's layouts: the firmware information (a build date of its era, hardware and firmware versions; a DualSense's build date is not checked, its place is not confirmed) and the motion sensors' calibration (there, both directions of every axis alike, in the USB or the Bluetooth order); said as a check, not a proof. On the board, the owner's DualShock 4 (CUH-ZCT1, Bluetooth) answered built 15 May 2015, hardware 0x5414, firmware 0x2033 (as the kernel logged them) and a symmetric calibration: like a genuine one. Find my account ID: while the dialog is open it takes the redirect address from the clipboard as soon as it is copied (only that address, not what was there before; also when the dialog has the focus again, as Wayland requires); the sign-in stays in the browser, so the app never has the password. Tests: the genuine check with the owner's controller's answers, the USB and adapter orders, a DualSense, eight ways a copy fails, other controllers; the test window with a stand-in for SDL; the clipboard; the button in the window.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 10:47:37

Steam's PS button setting in the app (owner, 13-10-2026): a checkbox under the registered consoles, Steam comes to the front on the PS button, shows and changes Steam's Guide Button Focus Steam (Settings > Controller > Show Advanced Settings). steam_settings.py: the setting is Controller_CheckGuideButton in each Steam user's userdata/<user>/config/localconfig.vdf (found on the owner's board, "0" after switching it off; on when missing); Steam writes that file when it exits, so the change closes Steam (steam -shutdown, after asking), writes every user's file (the line replaced or added, the rest of the file and its permissions kept) and starts Steam again in the background as the desktop does at login (bazzite-steam -silent on Bazzite). Steam's native folder and the Flatpak's are found. Hidden without Steam; disabled in Game Mode, where Steam keeps the PS button for its menu. Stream notes it in the log while it is on. The stream window's title masks the console's address when it has no name. Tests: reading and writing on Steam folders made in a temp folder, Steam running (closed, changed, started again), not closing (nothing changed), not running, Game Mode, the process check, the job, and the checkbox in the window (no question without Steam running, nothing changed on No).

Changelog: 13-10-2026 10:37:59

Privacy (owner's decisions, 13-10-2026): the PSN account ID, the PSN online ID and the consoles' addresses are masked in the window: the account ID shows only its last 4 characters, an address only its last part (•••.•••.•••.20), the online ID nothing. privacy.py has the masks and a Masker that hides the values it knows and, in free text, anything shaped like an IPv4 address, an account number or an account code. The window: the account ID and address fields show the masked value and are read-only while hidden (an empty one can be typed in and is masked once left), the address columns of both tables, the log (kept as it came, shown masked or in full) and the status badge's tooltip. A Show personal details button at the bottom shows them in full until switched off; every start begins masked. Whatever is copied from the log is masked, even while the window shows the details in full. The Stream button's tooltip no longer says there is no controller. The manual has a Privacy section: what is masked, the button, the copied log, and what the app sends to whom (the consoles, PSN only for Find my account ID, nobody else). Tests: the masks, the masker by value and by shape (an address at a sentence's end, version numbers left alone), and the window masked, shown, copied and hidden again.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 10:28:04

Rumble and the light bar (iteration 2): what the console sends for the controller reaches the controllers on the board. stream.py reads the rumble messages (data type 7: the left and right motor from 0 to 255) and the pad info (data type 9, the reference's two layouts of 25 and 17 bytes: the player number, the light bar's color and a request to reset the motion), counts them (stats rumble and pad_info; other_data now counts only the trigger effects) and hands them to drive_controllers' output, giving it the color and player number set so far at once. gamepad.py applies them on the reader's thread: the rumble to every controller through SDL (scaled to 16 bits, for 5 s unless renewed, as the reference does), the color and player number to every controller and to one connected later, and the motion reset as the reference's (the accelerometer as it is becomes the zero, less gravity; the tracker starts again from there). When the stream ends, every motor is stopped. Not yet: a DualSense's adaptive triggers (data type 11) and its own way of rumbling, and the rumble a PS5 sends as haptic sound for a controller without a DualSense's motors. Tests: both layouts, wrong sizes, the replay to a late output, the reader driving one controller and one connected later, the motor stop, the motion reset in the tracker and through the reader; on the board SDL accepted a rumble and a color for a DualShock 4.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 10:21:26

Motion (iteration 2): the controllers' gyro and accelerometer reach the console, with the orientation computed from them. orientation.rs, ported from the reference's orientation.c: Madgwick's IMU filter (a strong pull towards gravity for the first 30 samples, a weak one after), the input fuzz filter that holds it still against jitter, the 32-bit microsecond clock that may wrap, the accelerometer zero, and the turn back from Madgwick's frame; the same float and double steps as the reference's C, so every value is the same bits (tests/orientation_vectors.json, printed by the reference's own code: 120 samples through warm-up, a clock wrap, slow and fast turns, a sample without accelerometer). The native core gives it to Python as OrientationTracker. gamepad.py switches on each controller's sensors where it has them and takes their samples as SDL events, with the controller's own clock; each controller has its tracker, fed as the reference's GUI feeds it (an accelerometer sample in g with the last gyro values, a gyro sample with the last accelerometer values). Several controllers: the motion of the first one that moves, as the reference combines them. Differences: the samples are timed to the microsecond (the reference's GUI: whole milliseconds), and the state goes out once per 4 ms poll with every sample since taken in (the reference: after every sample). Without the native core, the log says the motion is not passed on. Tests: the tracker against the vectors in Rust and Python, the feeding and the units, the combining, the reader passing motion on, and a moving state reaching the fake console as the reference's bytes. On the board: SDL3 delivered about 156 samples per second per sensor from a DualShock 4, gravity about 1 g.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 09:40:13

Controller input (iteration 2): the controllers on the board play during Stream. gamepad.py reads them with the system's SDL3 through ctypes (no new Python package), on a thread of its own every 4 ms, from STREAMINFO until the stream ends, picking up controllers that come or go (looked for every second, each named in the log). The mapping is the reference's: SDL's south, east, west and north buttons as cross, circle, square and triangle, back as Share, start as Options, guide as PS, the touchpad click, the shoulders, stick clicks and the D-pad; L2 and R2 from 0 to 255; the sticks as they are; the touchpad's fingers with touch ids as the reference gives them out. Several controllers combine as the reference combines them. Deliberate difference: the fingers are scaled to the console's touchpad (1920 × 942 for a PS4, 1919 × 1079 for a PS5, the sizes the reference uses for its own touch input); the reference's gamepad path scales to 1920 × 1079 for both. A controller pulled out while a button is held is released for the console. Without SDL3 the log says so and the console sees a controller lying still. Not yet: the gyro and motion sensors (the reference's orientation tracker), rumble and the light bar. jobs.watch_stream takes the reader's backend (gamepads, SDL3 by default). Tests: the mapping, the axes, combining, the touchpad and its ids, the reader against a stand-in for SDL (changes sent once, controllers coming and going, no SDL), the real SDL3 opened and closed where installed, and Stream end to end with a controller holding cross, which reaches the fake console as the reference's button event. Also end to end: L1, R1, the right stick, a touch and the touchpad click, as the bytes the console decrypts. The manual says what controller to use and how to connect it.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 09:11:38

The controller state (iteration 2, controller input, first part): once the stream runs, the board sends the console the controller's state, at least every 200 ms, as the reference does; idle (nothing pressed, sticks centered, lying still) until something sets it. A PS4 Pro paused the stream without it ("Remote Play has paused"). feedback.rs, ported from the reference's feedback.c, feedbacksender.c, controller.c and the feedback packets of takion.c: FEEDBACK_STATE (sticks, gyro, accelerometer, the orientation compressed to 32 bits; 25 bytes for a PS4, 28 for a PS5) when the sticks or motion change and every 200 ms; FEEDBACK_HISTORY for buttons, L2/R2 and touches, one packet per event with the last eight repeated, the newest sent again after 20, 50, 100 and 200 ms; the payload encrypted and the packet signed with the local key. One difference: a gyro or accelerometer value outside its range is clamped (the reference's cast is undefined in C). The connection runs the sender on a thread of its own from start_stream; Takion.set_controller_state and StreamConnection.set_controller take a state, for the controller input to come. The log's summary counts the controller packets sent. Tests: every format against vectors printed by the reference's own C code (tests/feedback_vectors.json: states, button and touch events, the history of a sequence of states, sealed packets), the sender's timing, and the fake console decrypting the idle state every 200 ms and a button press with its repeats. The manual says it, and no longer says the picture is not shown.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 03:21:55

Sound (iteration 2): Stream plays the console's sound on the board's default audio output; M mutes it. audio.rs, the audio receiver ported from the reference: an audio packet's units into Opus frames (source units and FEC copies of the frames before), a jitter buffer of 8 that starts playing with 3, frames in order, a frame that never comes given up as a lost one. One difference: the FEC units are taken before the source units, so a lost frame comes back from the next packet's copy (the reference, taking the source units first, gives it up before it sees the copy while its buffer is nearly empty). The connection routes the audio through it once the stream runs (events "audio"; audio_stats). FFmpeg's own Opus decoder in the native core (AudioDecoder; the reference uses libopus), with libswresample, which it needs: native/ffmpeg/build.sh enables both and stops when a decoder is missing; build.rs links every library of that FFmpeg statically. A lost frame plays as 10 ms of silence (libopus would conceal it). sound.py keeps the sound between the stream and the sound card short (at most 100 ms, then the oldest goes, down to 40 ms; silence when empty); sound_out.py plays it with Qt Multimedia's QAudioSink. Test stream and the log count the sound's frames and losses. Tests: the receiver, the decoder against FFmpeg's decode of an Opus clip made with libopus (tests/audio_vectors.json, in Rust and Python), the buffer, the player without an audio output, and the fake console sending that clip with FEC copies, end to end to the window's sound.

A PS4 Pro's 1080p and the sound need the native core rebuilt with the new FFmpeg: from a clone, run native/ffmpeg/build.sh again, then install.sh.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 03:08:08

A PS4 Pro streams at 1080p (owner, 13-10-2026), at 15 Mbit/s as a PS5; a PS4 stays at 720p and 10 Mbit/s. The console says it is a PS4 Pro when the control connection opens, so jobs.stream_profile takes the server type. Tests: the profiles, and Test stream against a fake PS4 Pro and PS4 asking for 1080p and 720p. The manual says it.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 03:04:35

H.264 from every console, a PS5 too (owner, 13-10-2026): the BC-250 decodes in software, where HEVC takes about twice as long. It was already the session's default; jobs.STREAM_CODEC now says so, and the stream test checks that the launch spec asks a PS5 for avc. The manual says it.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:44:37

The picture on screen (iteration 2): a new button Stream opens a window with the console's picture and streams until the window closes (F11 or a double click for full screen, Escape back; a status line with the size and the pictures shown per second). video.py (without Qt): the decoded picture's planes (yuv420p from software decoding, nv12 from VA-API), its color matrix (the stream's BT.709, BT.601 or BT.2020; without one, BT.709 from 720 lines up, as FFmpeg's players assume) and range, and a slot that keeps only the newest picture between the stream's thread and the view. stream_view.py: the renderer (OpenGL 2.0, GLSL 1.10: the planes as luminance textures, Y'CbCr to RGB in a shader, the picture letterboxed at its aspect ratio), the view (QOpenGLWidget) and the window. The native decoder's image() gives a dict now, with the color matrix and range. The stream copies a picture out of the decoder only when the window has taken the last one; jobs.watch_stream runs the stream until the window closes (at most a second later) and Test stream shares its start with it. Not yet: sound, controller input, 10-bit (HDR) pictures. Tests: the shader drawn into an offscreen framebuffer and read back (colors of both formats and both ranges against the reference arithmetic, orientation, letterbox; skipped where no OpenGL context opens), the planes and matrices, the window, the Stream button, and watch_stream end to end with the fake consoles.

