Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:18:03

The video decoder (iteration 2, owner's choice 13-10-2026: FFmpeg bundled in the wheel CI builds, so installing stays pip only). native/ffmpeg/build.sh builds FFmpeg 7.1.2 from its pinned sources (signature checked when pinned, checksum checked on every build), static and LGPL only: the H.264 and HEVC decoders with VA-API, nothing else; libva stays the system's, linked dynamically (without its headers, as on Bazzite, they come from libva 2.22.0's pinned sources). The cargo feature "decoder" links it (build.rs, decoder.c: a small C interface over libavcodec, decoder.rs over that); Python gets Decoder, DECODER and FFMPEG_VERSION. The stream opens the decoder at STREAMINFO and decodes every frame; the resolution's header goes in front of the next frame (the reference sends it alone, which FFmpeg's H.264 decoder refuses). VA-API when the GPU has a video decoder, else software: the BC-250 (Cyan Skillfish) has no VCN, so on the board it is software; measured there, one thread with FFmpeg's assembly: 3.6 ms per 720p H.264 frame, 6.6 ms at 1080p, 13.9 ms for 1080p HEVC (without nasm 6.3, 13.2 and 38.5 ms). Test stream reports the frames decoded and how; the log names the decoder and the time per frame. install.sh installs the wheel a release brings (native/wheel), and from a clone builds the core with the decoder when build.sh has built FFmpeg. FFmpeg's LGPL text in native/ffmpeg. Tests: H.264 and HEVC clips made with x264 and x265 (tests/video_vectors.json) decode to the same pictures as the system's FFmpeg, in Rust and Python; the fake console streams those clips in the codec the launch spec asks for.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 02:02:34

Release 0.1.3.

Changelog: 13-10-2026 01:53:29

The video frames in the native core (iteration 2): fec.rs, the Cauchy Reed-Solomon FEC over GF(2^8) that the reference gets from Jerasure, written from its definition (polynomial 0x11d, Jerasure's original Cauchy matrix) and checked against eight frames recorded by the reference (tests/fec_vectors.json, from its test/fec_test_cases.inl; our encoder also gives the console's FEC units); frame.rs, the frame processor (units into frames, FEC when units are missing) and the packet counts; video.rs, the video receiver (frame order, flush when enough units are there, CORRUPTFRAME for frames that never came or could not be rebuilt); and the congestion reports every 200 ms, the loss reported at most 5 %. The connection starts all of it with start_stream at STREAMINFO; from then on video comes out as whole frames, and the Python side sends the CORRUPTFRAME reports. The reference's arithmetic kept as it is: a frame without FEC units counts one FEC unit expected, and the audio counts take the sequence difference as lost when more packets came than it. Differences: a frame whose first packet is invalid is empty here (the reference keeps the last frame's state). Not yet: the reference's look into the bitstream (a missing reference frame swapped for an older one, a key frame asked for after a failed FEC), which comes with the decoder. Test stream now reports frames, frames rebuilt with FEC, frames lost and the video bitrate; the log has the video receiver's counts. Python: fec_encode and fec_decode, Takion.start_stream and video_stats, the events "frame", "profile" and "corrupt". Tests: the FEC, frame and video modules, a connection test with a lost unit, and the fake console sends real frames with FEC units and can lose units and frames.

