Changelog: 13-10-2026 00:47:43

The network test before the stream, Senkusha (senkusha.py, from the reference's senkusha.c): a Takion connection of version 7 to UDP 9297 without crypto, the protocol request and BIG, ten pings for the round trip, then a binary search for the MTU in (the console sends video packets of the size asked) and out (the board sends pings of that size). Test stream runs it first and puts its MTU and round trip into the launch spec (MTU in) and the BIG's split (the smaller MTU); when it fails, the stream uses the reference's values for that case (1454 bytes, 1 ms), as the reference does. Its messages equal the reference's nanopb encodings (tests/stream_vectors.json): the reference's MTU command sets num without its has-flag, so num is never sent, and neither is it here. The native core has the version 7 header (v7_av_header, equal to the reference's takion.c, which writes the key position in the machine's byte order where every other field is big-endian), send_raw for the pings, and Takion connections without crypto, where video and audio are not held for keys. As in the reference, an MTU-out search that does not start at 1454 ends at 1453 at most. Tests: the console's side of the network test (SenkushaConsole in tests/fake_stream.py, with MTU limits standing in for the network), tests/test_senkusha.py, the job with the network test and without it, and the v7 header in Rust and from Python.

